## Answer Rules

- Write short, to-the-point narratives instead of bullet points or enumerations. Use them only when the content requires it (e.g., listing steps).
- Write all replies using Simplified Technical English (STE) principles. Use short, complete sentences and active voice. Use the same term for the same concept. Give direct instructions. Avoid idioms and unnecessary words. Preserve technical accuracy, code, commands, and identifiers. Do not sacrifice grammar for brevity.

## Coding Rules

- When writing or reviewing code, follow the YAGNI principle and prefer one-line solutions when they stay readable.
- When a file exceeds 600 lines, it must be split.
- Do not implement large functions that exceed 100 lines or reach 4 levels of nesting.
- **NEVER** change the README unless the user tells you to do so.

## Planning Rules

- Use diagrams, charts, and code samples.
- Explain which files will be touched.
- Explain the package structure if the change is large.

